r the dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance\" (Implementation, P1)\n- BRANCH-B-E006: \"Tracking server artifact-operations delegation configuration\" (Implementation, P1, branch B)\n\nNow the epic bodies. Must contain Title (one line), Description, Scope, Acceptance Criteria, HLR Traceability. No sibling epic ID references (\"E001\" etc.). Careful: don't write \"RHAISTRAT-8009-E00x\" in bodies.\n\nLet me draft each file content.\n\n**E001 body:**\n\n# Investigate plugin workspace resolution dependencies in the artifacts-only context\n\n## Description\nResolve whether the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution (workspace store initialization and experiment-to-workspace mapping) can function in an MLflow artifacts-only server context where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. The strategy flags this as a genuine unknown: if workspace resolution is currently coupled to the full tracking server initialization path, the plugin requires refactoring before workspace-aware artifact serving can work. The answer determines the scope and approach of the plugin extension work and of the server-side workspace validation.\n\n## Scope\n- Analyze the plugin's workspace provider initialization path (registered via the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group and loaded by `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`) to identify any dependency on tracking store or model registry store initialization.\n- Determine whether the `kubernetes://` workspace store provides experiment-to-workspace mapping accessible without tracking stores.\n- Build a minimal reproduction/test harness that exercises workspace resolution in an artifacts-only server context (tracking and registry stores uninitialized).\n- If coupling is found: document the specific initialization dependencies and recommend a refactor approach that factors workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin.\n- Record a bounded outcome: (A) workspace resolution is independent — verification and tests only; (B) workspace resolution is coupled — refactor required.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A written findings report states, with code-level evidence, whether workspace resolution initializes and resolves experiment-to-workspace mapping without tracking store or model registry store initialization.\n- A runnable reproduction demonstrates workspace resolution succeeding or failing in the artifacts-only context (tracking and registry stores uninitialized).\n- If coupling exists, the report enumerates each initialization dependency and a recommended independent-initialization refactor approach.\n- The bounded outcome (independent vs coupled) is recorded with the evidence that selects it.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P1] Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization (gating investigation — determines scope of the work that delivers this requirement)\n- Strategy assumption \"workspace store provides experiment-to-workspace mapping accessible without initializing tracking stores — needs validation\" (resolved by this investigation)\n\n**E002 body:**\n\n# Extend plugin workspace resolution for the artifacts-only server context\n\n## Description\nVerify and extend the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin so that workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only MLflow server context, where tracking stores and model registry stores are not initialized. The artifacts-only proxy calls the same workspace resolution logic the tracking server uses for non-artifact operations, before proxying artifact requests to the storage backend.\n\n## Scope\n- Verify workspace resolution (workspace provider initialization, experiment-to-workspace lookup) in the artifacts-only server context per the investigation outcome.\n- If the investigation finds coupling to the full tracking server initialization path: factor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin.\n- Ensure resolution failures (e.g., unreachable workspace store) surface as errors rather than silent successes, supporting fail-closed behavior in the server.\n- Preserve existing workspace resolution behavior for the tracking server's non-artifact operations (no regression).\n- Unit and integration tests covering workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Workspace resolution initializes and resolves experiment-to-workspace mapping in an artifacts-only context with tracking and model registry stores uninitialized, demonstrated by passing tests.\n- Existing workspace resolution tests for the tracking server context pass unchanged (no regression to non-artifact operations).\n- Workspace store unavailability during resolution produces an error result, not a successful/undefined response.\n- Any refactor keeps the plugin's registration surface (`mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point, `WorkspaceStoreRegistry` loading) compatible with the tracking server's existing usage.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P1] Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization\n- Supports (as prerequisite) the P0 requirements for workspace-validated artifact serving and fail-closed behavior\n\n**E003 body:**\n\n# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n\n## Description\nMake the MLflow server's `--artifacts-only` mode composable with `--enable-workspaces` so that the artifacts-only server initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, resolves each artifact request's path to its parent experiment, looks up the experiment's workspace assignment, and delegates authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure. Requests failing workspace validation are denied; the design is fail-closed.\n\n## Scope\n- Flag composition: allow `--artifacts-only` to combine with `--enable-workspaces`; require `--workspace-store-uri` when both are set (fail to start with a clear error if missing).\n- Initialize only the workspace provider in artifacts-only + workspaces mode; tracking stores and model registry stores stay uninitialized; non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n- Workspace validation of artifact requests: resolve artifact path to parent experiment (S3 artifact store path structure `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` for the initial implementation), look up workspace assignment via the workspace store, delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure; denials return HTTP 403.\n- Fail-closed behavior: if the workspace store is unreachable while workspace-aware mode is active, deny requests rather than serving without validation (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation unavailable).\n- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` retains current behavior; single-tenant deployments and configurations without workspace flags are unchanged; no existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys change meaning.\n- Audit logging of workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs.\n- Document limitations for non-standard artifact roots (initial implementation scoped to the S3 artifact store).\n- Measure per-request workspace validation overhead and document the result (informs the product decision on caching; performance optimization is out of scope for this strategy).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Server starts with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` and initializes only the workspace provider; tracking and model registry stores remain uninitialized and non-artifact endpoints remain disabled.\n- Server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` but without `--workspace-store-uri` fails to start with a clear error message.\n- A user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact of an experiment in workspace A receives HTTP 200 with the correct artifact content.\n- A user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact of an experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n- With the workspace store unreachable, artifact requests are denied (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation unavailable); no artifact is served without validation.\n- With `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated user's artifact request is served without workspace validation (current behavior, HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership).\n- Workspace access denials in artifact serving are recorded in server logs.\n- Per-request workspace validation latency overhead is measured and documented.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P0] `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` to enable workspace-validated artifact serving\n- [P0] Artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against workspace membership; requests from users without workspace membership are denied with HTTP 403\n- [P0] Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable, requests are denied\n- [P0] Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior with no changes\n- [P2] Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (bundled: same artifact request-handling surface as the P0 validation work; splitting would build the same surface twice)\n\n**E004 body:**\n\n# Investigate artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology\n\n## Description\nDetermine how the MLflow operator should configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance in the split-server topology. The strategy flags this as an open question: the answer affects the HTTPRoute design and determines whether the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations. The outcome selects between bounded alternatives and gates the operator's routing implementation.\n\n## Scope\n- Evaluate routing alternatives: (A) gateway-level routing via Gateway API HTTPRoute path/host rules directing artifact API traffic to the dedicated instance, with no tracking server change; (B) tracking server configuration change to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance, wired by the operator.\n- Assess HTTPRoute design implications (path matching for artifact endpoints, interaction with the operator's existing conditional CRD discovery pattern for Gateway API).\n- Determine whether the main tracking server/deployment requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations, and what that configuration surface would be.\n- Record a bounded recommendation with rationale and the evidence that selects it.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A written design recommendation documents the selected routing approach with rationale and evaluation of the bounded alternatives.\n- The recommendation explicitly states whether the tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations (outcome A: no server change; outcome B: server-side delegation configuration required).\n- HTTPRoute design implications, including behavior under the operator's conditional Gateway API CRD discovery, are documented.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P1] Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance (gating investigation — determines the design that delivers this requirement)\n\n**E005 body:**\n\n# Operator split-server topology for the dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance\n\n## Description\nExtend the MLflow operator to deploy a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute (split-server topology), and configure the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to that dedicated instance. The operator uses its existing Helm-in-operator pattern: MLflow CR spec converted to Helm values, rendered through embedded chart templates, applied via Server-Side Apply.\n\n## Scope\n- New Helm chart templates for the artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute.\n- Updates to the main tracking Deployment configuration to route artifact traffic to the dedicated instance per the selected routing design.\n- Propagation of workspace configuration (`--enable-workspaces`, `--workspace-store-uri`, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`) to the artifacts-only instance using the same configuration mechanisms as the main tracking deployment.\n- HTTPRoute resources created conditionally based on runtime CRD discovery, consistent with the operator's existing pattern for optional dependencies.\n- Operator validation rejecting configurations where the artifacts-only instance lacks workspace flags when workspace-aware artifact serving is enabled.\n- Backwards compatibility: MLflow CRs without split-server artifact serving reconcile unchanged.\n- The dedicated instance runs the workspace-aware artifacts-only server with the combined flag set (`--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts`) — coordinated with the server capability rather than sequenced behind it.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Reconciling an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving creates a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute separate from the main tracking Deployment.\n- The artifacts-only Deployment's container command includes `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts`.\n- The main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance per the selected routing design.\n- An MLflow CR specifying the artifacts-only instance without workspace flags 