{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b10fcae391a65d9f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8009 — Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n11\t\n12\t## Triage\n13\t\n14\tNot below-threshold: the strategy is M-L effort (4-7 sprints), spans three affected components\n15\t(MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) with architecturally distinct work streams\n16\t(Python server changes, Go operator topology changes, plugin verification/extension), and\n17\tcontains genuine unknowns flagged as open questions. Not documentation-only: all affected\n18\tcomponents require code changes. Full decomposition applies.\n19\t\n20\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides (Step 1.5)\n21\t\n22\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input is non-empty and takes precedence over the AI-generated\n23\tTechnical Approach. The strategy body already incorporates the corrections; the decomposition\n24\tfollows them. Overrides logged for traceability:\n25\t\n26\t1. **No new flag** — the original STRAT proposed a `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag and a\n27\t   `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field; the SME corrected this to combining the existing\n28\t   `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` flags. The server epic's scope uses only the\n29\t   existing flags; the operator epic propagates the existing flag set. No new server flag or CR\n30\t   field appears in any epic.\n31\t2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle** — the operator epic is scoped as\n32\t   deploying a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service\n33\t   and HTTPRoute, plus artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment.\n34\t3. **SSAR detail is over-specified** — no epic prescribes SelfSubjectAccessReview or other\n35\t   Kubernetes API mechanics for workspace authorization; the epics reference the existing auth\n36\t   plugin infrastructure (`--app-name kubernetes-auth`, workspace store) without dictating\n37\t   implementation mechanics.\n38\t\n39\t## Component Mapping (canonical component constraint)\n40\t\n41\t`.context/rhai-components.txt` contains `MLflow` but no `MLflow Operator` or\n42\t`mlflow-kubernetes-plugins`. All epics therefore use `component: MLflow` (the closest and only\n43\tcanonical name covering this stack):\n44\t\n45\t| Strategy component | Canonical component | Notes |\n46\t|---|---|---|\n47\t| MLflow | MLflow | direct match |\n48\t| MLflow Operator | MLflow | operator work (Go, red-hat-data-services/mlflow-operator) filed under MLflow |\n49\t| mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | MLflow | plugin work (Python package) filed under MLflow |\n50\t\n51\tAll three strategy components exist in the platform: `mlflow.md` and `mlflow-operator.md`\n52\tarchitecture context files exist (RHOAI 3.6-ea.1), and `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` is documented\n53\twithin `mlflow.md` as an existing package (v1.5.0, `kubernetes-auth` plugin) with the strategy's\n54\tdependencies table marking it \"internal, exists\". No repo/konflux/license onboarding chain is\n55\tgenerated — the components already have repositories and Konflux pipelines per architecture\n56\tcontext (recent-change entries show konflux-central pipeline syncs).\n57\t\n58\t## Epic List\n59\t\n60\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n61\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n62\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigate plugin workspace resolution dependencies in the artifacts-only context | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n63\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Extend plugin workspace resolution for the artifacts-only server context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n64\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n65\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Investigate artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n66\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Operator split-server topology for the dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n67\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E006 | Tracking server artifact-operations delegation configuration | Implementation (branch B) | MLflow Team | P1 |\n68\t\n69\tE001 and E002 are P0 by priority inheritance: they transitively enable the P0 workspace-validated\n70\tartifact serving capability (the plugin HLR is labeled P1 in the strategy, but the plugin\n71\tresolution is a prerequisite of the P0 server behavior — see Health Warnings).\n72\t\n73\t## Dependency DAG\n74\t\n75\t```mermaid\n76\tgraph TD\n77\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: plugin workspace resolution dependencies\"]\n78\t    E002[\"E002 Implementation: plugin workspace resolution extension\"]\n79\t    E003[\"E003 Implementation: workspace-aware artifacts-only serving\"]\n80\t    E004[\"E004 Investigation: artifact traffic routing design\"]\n81\t    E005[\"E005 Implementation: operator split-server topology\"]\n82\t    B006[\"BRANCH-B-E006 branch: server delegation config\"]\n83\t\n84\t    E001 --> E002\n85\t    E001 --> E003\n86\t    E002 --> E003\n87\t    E004 --> E005\n88\t    E004 --> B006\n89\t    E003 --> B006\n90\t```\n91\t\n92\tMain-plan critical path: E001 → E002 → E003 (length 3). The two chains (server/plugin chain and\n93\toperator chain) are independent and run in parallel. Branch epic BRANCH-B-E006 is excluded from\n94\tthe main-plan critical path.\n95\t\n96\t## DAG Justification\n97\t\n98\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n99\t|---|---|---|\n100\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The plugin-coupling investigation determines whether the plugin extension is verification-only or requires refactoring workspace resolution into an independent initialization path — scope/approach change. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n101\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The server's workspace validation initializes the workspace provider; the shape of that initialization depends on whether resolution is coupled to tracking store initialization. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n102\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 12 (library → consumer) | The server's artifact-request validation calls the plugin's workspace resolution; the server builds against the plugin's resolution surface, so the plugin work is serial-first. |\n103\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The routing-design investigation determines the HTTPRoute design and the routing implementation approach in the operator. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n104\t| E004 → BRANCH-B-E006 | Rule 3 (bounded outcomes) | The server-side delegation epic exists only under outcome B (tracking server needs a configuration change); under outcome A it does not exist. `remove` on the other outcome. |\n105\t| E003 → BRANCH-B-E006 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer) | The server-side delegation configuration builds on the server implementation surface delivered by the workspace-aware artifacts-only serving work. |\n106\t| (no edge) E003 → E005 | Rule 13/14 (config reference, different repos) | The operator consumes the server as a deployed image configured with a known flag set — a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact. The coupling is expressed as an acceptance criterion on the operator epic (the dedicated instance runs the workspace-aware artifacts-only server with the combined flag set), never as a DAG edge. The operator and server work run in parallel in different repos. |\n107\t| (no edge) E001/E002/E003 ↔ E004/E005 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared artifacts) | The server/plugin chain and the operator chain are independent work streams; no shared build artifacts. |\n108\t\n109\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n110\t\n111\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n112\t|---|---|---|\n113\t| `--artifacts-only` can be combined with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` to enable workspace-validated artifact serving | P0 | E003 |\n114\t| Artifact requests through the workspace-aware artifacts-only server are validated against workspace membership; non-members denied with HTTP 403 | P0 | E003 (enabled by E002) |\n115\t| Workspace-aware artifact serving is fail-closed: workspace store unreachable → requests denied | P0 | E003 (enabled by E002) |\n116\t| Single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior | P0 | E003 |\n117\t| MLflow operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute | P1 | E005 |\n118\t| Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance | P1 | E005 (+ BRANCH-B-E006 under routing-design outcome B) |\n119\t| Auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization | P1 | E002 (gated by E001) |\n120\t| Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs | P2 | E003 (bundled) |\n121\t\n122\tThe P2 audit-logging HLR is bundled into E003 rather than split: it is logged in the same\n123\tartifact request-handling surface that enforces the P0 403 denials — the same code path, same\n124\tAPI endpoints, same component and team. Splitting it would create two epics building the same\n125\trequest-handling surface plus an artificial serialization edge; it is incidental to the P0 work,\n126\tnot a deferrable distinct feature.\n127\t\n128\t## Unknowns Disposition (Step 3 / Step 6.5)\n129\t\n130\t| Unknown (source) | Disposition | Rationale |\n131\t|---|---|---|\n132\t| Does workspace store initialization in mlflow-kubernetes-plugins depend on tracking store availability? (Open Questions; assumption \"workspace store accessible without tracking stores — needs validation\") | Investigation E001 | Answer changes the scope/approach of the plugin extension and the server's provider initialization (rewrite). Two flagged unknowns (open question + assumption) resolved by the same experiment — combined into one Investigation. |\n133\t| How should the operator configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking server to the dedicated instance? (Open Questions) | Investigation E004, conditional branch | Bounded outcomes (≤3): (A) gateway-level HTTPRoute routing, no server change; (B) tracking server needs a delegation configuration change. Outcome B adds a server-side epic → conditional branch BRANCH-B-E006. |\n134\t| What is the acceptable latency overhead for workspace validation per request? (Open Questions, PM/Engineering) | Health warning + AC on E003 | The strategy's Out-of-Scope excludes streaming/performance optimization (separate RFE), and no latency target is specified — the answer affects a follow-up RFE, not which epics exist here. E003 carries an AC to measure and document per-request validation overhead so the PM decision is informed. |\n135\t| Should workspace access denials generate audit events through a specific telemetry channel? (Open Questions, PM/Security) | Health warning + AC on E003 | The P2 HLR already specifies \"through server logs\"; E003 implements that. A PM/Security decision to use a different channel (OTLP, Kubernetes audit log) would change E003 scope — flagged, not branched. |\n136\t| Flags composable without breaking either flag used individually (assumption, needs validation) | AC on E003 | The server epic's charter is precisely to make them composable; backwards-compatibility ACs cover both flags used individually. Implementation detail, not a structural unknown. |\n137\t| Single-tenant deployments do not set `--enable-workspaces` (assumption, needs validation) | AC on E003 | Behavior-preservation requirement; covered by the no-workspace-flags regression AC. |\n138\t| Operator Helm chart supports a second Deployment without structural changes (assumption, needs validation) | AC/early validation within E005 | The operator epic exists and delivers the same topology either way; discovering structural chart work is within the epic's own scope, not a structural unknown. |\n139\t| Artifact path → experiment resolution feasibility (risk) | Scoped AC on E003 | Initial implementation scoped to the S3 artifact store path structure `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`; limitations for non-standard artifact roots documented. |\n140\t| SSAR mechanics for workspace authorization (de-specified by Staff Engineer) | Dropped per SME input | Implementation detail within the plugin, deliberately not strategy-level. |\n141\t\n142\t## Health Warnings\n143\t\n144\t1. **Priority label inversion (non-blocking)**: the plugin HLR is labeled P1 in the strategy, but\n145\t   plugin workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context is a prerequisite of all four P0\n146\t   HLRs — the plugin epic and its gating investigation are decomposed P0 by priority\n147\t   inheritance. The strategy's P1 label understates the plugin work's structural role.\n148\t2. **Latency open question (PM/Engineering)**: no latency target is specified; performance\n149\t   optimization is out of scope (separate RFE). E003 measures and documents per-request\n150\t   validation overhead. If the PM decision requires short-TTL caching at launch, that is a scope\n151\t   addition to the server work (the risk-mitigation caching pattern is noted there), not a new\n152\t   epic.\n153\t3. **Audit telemetry channel (PM/Security)**: the P2 HLR specifies server logs; if PM/Security\n154\t   select OTLP or the Kubernetes audit log instead, the audit-logging scope in E003 changes\n155\t   (channel, format, review criteria).\n156\t4. **Pending process gates**: all Prerequisites & Process Gates (Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI\n157\t   Build Onboarding, License Validation, Accelerator/Package Support, Documentation Support, UXD,\n158\t   Performance Team) are \"Pending human review\" at the strategy level. No onboarding epics were\n159\t   generated because all components already have repositories and Konflux pipelines per\n160\t   architecture context; the pending human reviews remain strategy-level gates outside this\n161\t   decomposition's control.\n162\t5. **Artifact path resolution risk**: initial implementation is scoped to the S3 artifact store\n163\t   (standard RHOAI configuration) with its well-defined `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`\n164\t   structure; non-standard artifact roots are documented as a limitation, not supported.\n165\t6. **Design artifacts not yet authored**: the strategy lists Design doc \"To be created\" and ADR\n166\t   \"To be created if needed\". No epic is generated for these (not substantive user-facing\n167\t   documentation; `Documentation Support` is not \"Yes\"); the design decisions surface through the\n168\t   two Investigations instead.\n169\t\n170\t## Tiered Delivery\n171\t\n172\tNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and existing (workspace store, auth plugin,\n173\tGateway API, kube-rbac-proxy per the strategy's dependencies table); there is no external\n174\tdependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split is needed. The two independent\n175\tchains (server/plugin and operator) provide natural parallelism instead.\n176\t\n177\t## Conditional Decomposition (Branches)\n178\t\n179\tInvestigation E004 (artifact traffic routing design) has bounded outcomes:\n180\t\n181\t- **Outcome A — gateway-level routing**: Gateway API HTTPRoute path/host rules direct artifact\n182\t  API traffic to the dedicated instance; no tracking server change. The operator epic's routing\n183\t  scope covers this entirely; no branch epic exists.\n184\t- **Outcome B — server-side delegation required**: the tracking server needs a configuration\n185\t  change to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance. Branch epic\n186\t  **RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E006** (Tracking server artifact-operations delegation\n187\t  configuration, P1, component MLflow, MLflow Team) delivers the server-side configuration\n188\t  surface; it is gated by E004 (`gated_by`, `dependencies` include E004 and E003) with\n189\t  `gate_failure_impact.action=remove` — if outcome A holds, the epic does not exist.\n190\t\n191\tBranch epics are attached to the gating Investigation as a plan document, not created as\n192\tstandalone Jira issues; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its dependencies.\n193\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-918861ee198b169f","agent_id":"a259ae75130f24d6c","session_id":"52e2d593-99fe-4673-8bf8-9749f85fbc69","uuid":"ad4c01f4-4dd8-436e-a05b-bf8e1092ef9a","timestamp":"2026-10-23T16:15:00.218Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b10fcae391a65d9f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
