{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-8ff4a724-734d-4a9d-9fc7-40291290f241","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-86eb6d43ccfe4f63","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-xlim_tl5/artifacts/epic-reviews/RHAISTRAT-8009-decomp-review.md","content":"# Decomposition Review: RHAISTRAT-8009\n\n## Review Summary\n\nScore: 12/14 — fail\nRecommendation: revise\n\nContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py` exited 0 — body sections, canonical\ncomponent names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, and Rules 7/8/10\nparallelism all pass). Triage consistency: `triage: null` (full decomposition), so the\n6-epic multi-chain output is expected and not penalized.\n\nThe decomposition is strong on six of seven criteria: complete HLR traceability with\ncorrect priority inheritance, a coherent DAG whose frontmatter matches the summary\ndiagram edge-for-edge, correct Investigation gating metadata, well-calibrated AI\nsignals whose citations verify against `.context`, testable ACs, and full scope\ncoverage with every risk/assumption/open question dispositioned. It fails on one\ndimension: E005 bundles the entire operator split-server topology into a single epic\nthat the strategy's own effort estimate sizes at 2-3 sprints — a clearly oversized,\nmultiple-sprint epic that should be split.\n\n## Criterion Details\n\n### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n\nAll eight HLRs map to epics in the traceability matrix, and each epic's HLR\nTraceability section confirms the mapping: the four P0 HLRs (flag composition, 403\nworkspace validation, fail-closed, single-tenant/no-flags preservation) all land in\nE003; the P1 operator HLRs in E005 (+ BRANCH-B-E006 for the server-side routing\nportion); the P1 plugin HLR in E002 (gated by E001); the P2 audit-logging HLR in E003.\n\nPriority inheritance is correct in both directions. E001 and E002 are P0 because they\ntransitively gate all P0 implementation work (E003) — the summary's Health Warning 1\ndocuments this inversion of the strategy's P1 label on the plugin HLR explicitly. E004\nis P1 and gates only P1 work (E005, BRANCH-B-E006), so no lower-priority epic blocks\nP0 work. No docs-authoring epics exist, so the docs exemption is not in play.\n\nPriority collapse check: the P2 audit-logging HLR bundled into E003 is justified — it\nis logged in the same request-handling code path that enforces the 403 denials (same\nsurface, same component, same team), which is incidental polish on the P0 work, not a\ndeferrable distinct feature. The summary argues exactly this. E002's P1 HLR at P0\npriority is inheritance, not collapse.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n\nNo circular dependencies; no invalid edges. The mermaid diagram and epic frontmatter\n`dependencies` match edge-for-edge in both directions (E001→E002, E001→E003, E002→E003,\nE004→E005, E004→B006, E003→B006), with arrows drawn dependency→dependent per\nconvention. Every Investigation dependency (E002/E003 on E001; E005/B006 on E004)\ncarries `gated_by` pointing at an epic in that epic's direct `dependencies` list.\n\nBranch handling is correct: BRANCH-B-E006 sets both `branch: B` (matching the\nfilename) and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E004`; no main-plan epic lists the branch epic\nin its dependencies; the branch is attached as a plan document per the summary.\n\nThe E002→E003 edge (plugin → server) is justified: the server's artifact-request\nvalidation builds against the plugin's resolution surface. The absent E003→E005 edge\nis the rule-compliant shape, not a missing data dependency: the operator consumes the\nworkspace-aware server as a deployed image configured with a known flag set — a\nconfiguration reference (Rule 13) correctly expressed as ACs on E005 (the\ncontainer-command flag-set AC and the end-to-end AC) rather than a DAG edge, keeping\nthe two chains in parallel across different repos. Critical path 3 (E001→E002→E003)\nis reasonable for a two-parallel-chain M-L strategy; effort is spread in width, not\ndepth.\n\nNote (no score reduction; root cause counted under Criterion 3): the E004→E005 gate\nis broader than the dependency it justifies — only E005's routing-configuration scope\ndepends on the routing-design outcome, while the dedicated-instance Deployment and\nService templates do not. This is a consequence of the E005 boundary problem below,\nnot an independent edge defect.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (0/2)\n\nComponent/team mapping is correct: `MLflow` is the only canonical component name in\n`.context/rhai-components.txt` covering this stack (no `MLflow Operator` or\n`mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` entries), and all epics consistently use `component:\nMLflow` / `team: MLflow Team` with the mapping documented in the summary. No\ncross-team bundling. No scope duplication between siblings: E002 (plugin) and E003\n(server) are different codebases and surfaces; E005 (operator-side routing) and\nBRANCH-B-E006 (server-side delegation config) are different surfaces of the routing\noutcome. No work-product type mixing (the \"document limitations\" item in E003 is\nincidental to the code change, not a content-authoring deliverable).\n\nHowever, **E005 is clearly oversized**. The strategy's own effort estimate sizes the\noperator split-deployment topology — \"dedicated Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute,\nartifact traffic routing\" — at **2-3 sprints**, and E005's scope matches that work\nexactly (new Helm templates for three resource types, main-deployment routing\nupdates, workspace flag propagation, conditional CRD-discovery wiring, CR validation,\nbackwards compatibility, and an end-to-end multi-tenant AC through the deployed split\ntopology). A 2-3 sprint epic is multiple sprints of work; it cannot be completed in a\nsingle sprint, so it cannot even score at the \"slightly oversized\" level. This is a\nmajor issue and forces this criterion to 0.\n\nThe split axis is natural and the decomposition itself hints at it: only the\nartifact-traffic-routing portion of E005 depends on the E004 routing-design outcome,\nso E005 should be split into (a) dedicated artifacts-only instance deployment\n(Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute templates, workspace flag propagation, CR validation,\nbackwards compat) and (b) artifact-traffic routing configuration from the main\ntracking deployment. The split also removes the over-broad E004 gate: the deployment\nwork could proceed in parallel with the routing investigation.\n\nE003 (server-side work, strategy estimate 1-2 sprints) is borderline on size but\nacceptable — one logical change to one request-handling surface, plausibly a single\nsprint at the low end of the estimate, and the P2 audit logging bundled into it is\nthe same code path.\n\nMinor: BRANCH-B-E006's scope item \"Ensure the operator's rendering of the main\ntracking deployment can set this configuration\" crosses into E005's operator-side\nrouting scope; under outcome B both epics claim the main-tracking-deployment wiring.\nClarify which epic delivers the operator-side wiring.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nBoth Investigations are genuine structural gates. E001's outcome (independent vs\ncoupled workspace resolution) changes E002's scope between verification-only and a\nrefactor, and changes E003's provider-initialization approach — both downstream epics\ncarry `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` with a concrete fallback approach. E004's\noutcome selects whether BRANCH-B-E006 exists at all (`action=remove`) and changes\nE005's routing implementation. Neither investigation is disguised implementation\nwork; both produce decision artifacts that determine downstream structure.\n\nAll four gated epics (E002, E003, E005, BRANCH-B-E006) have both `gated_by` and a\n`gate_failure_impact` with `action` and `fallback_approach` populated, and every\n`gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies`. No ungated\nInvestigation dependencies exist. All Implementation epics produce concrete code\nartifacts with tests.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nSignal sets are type-correct: E001/E004 carry `investigation_signals` (5-signal\nrubric); E002/E003/E005/BRANCH-B-E006 carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric). All six\nai-signals rationale files exist and their rationales match the frontmatter values.\n\nCalibration was verified against `.context` rather than taken on internal consistency\nalone:\n\n- `architecture_claims: +1` on all four Implementation epics, each citing a context\n  file that actually corroborates the claim: mlflow.md documents the deployed CMD\n  flag set (`--app-name kubernetes-auth --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri\n  kubernetes:// --serve-artifacts`) and the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins v1.5.0 package;\n  mlflow-operator.md documents the Helm-in-operator pattern, Server-Side Apply, and\n  conditional Gateway API CRD discovery. This is the calibrated shape — no\n  \"no architecture claims\" misses.\n- `repo_access` values are honest and verified: E003/BRANCH-B-E006 cite\n  red-hat-data-services/mlflow and E005 cites red-hat-data-services/mlflow-operator,\n  both documented as repositories in the respective context files; E002 scores 0\n  because mlflow.md documents the plugins package only as PyPI-installed with no\n  source repository — accurate.\n- Investigation signals are consistent with the rubric's polarity and conditions:\n  both questions are specific with defined selection criteria (+1), answers are\n  determinable from readable source (+1 source_accessibility — correctly scoring the\n  operator's generated templates as source per the rubric note), end-to-end\n  verification needs a live cluster (local_runnability 0, cluster_hardware_dependence\n  -1 for peripheral-only dependence), and no stakeholder decision gates either\n  question (human_judgment_required 0).\n\nBRANCH-B-E006's lower change_specificity/pattern_precedent/adapter_pattern scores (0)\nwith the gated-scope rationale are appropriately calibrated for an epic whose\nconcrete surface is unknown until the investigation selects it.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy: E003's ACs operationalize\nstrategy acceptance criteria 1-4 (member 200, non-member 403, no-workspace-flags\n200, unreachable-store 503 fail-closed) plus the missing-store-uri startup error and\nthe audit-logging and latency-measurement items; E005's ACs operationalize strategy\ncriteria 5-6 (dedicated Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute presence, exact container\ncommand flag set) plus CR validation rejection and backwards compatibility.\n\nRule-mandated ACs: the flag composition itself is the feature gate for E003's\nbehavior change, and the backwards-compatibility ACs cover both flags used\nindividually and CRs without split-server serving (E005) — the replacement/rollback\nintent is satisfied. No konflux-chain or docs-authoring epics exist, so those\nmandated ACs are not applicable.\n\nNo non-testable ACs: every AC conditions on what the epic itself builds or verifies;\nnone states an externally-controlled schedule or availability precondition. No\ndraft-ID references appear in any epic body — cross-epic references use descriptive\nphrases (\"the workspace-resolution dependency investigation\", \"the selected routing\ndesign\"), with epic IDs confined to frontmatter.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nAll strategy scope is accounted for across the epic set: the three affected\ncomponents map to E002 (plugin), E003 (server), and E005/BRANCH-B-E006 (operator +\nconditional server delegation); all six strategy acceptance criteria have\ncorresponding epic ACs; the P2 audit logging, the S3-scoped artifact path resolution,\nand the non-standard-artifact-root limitation documentation all appear in E003's\nscope.\n\nStrategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: all five\n\"needs validation\" assumptions and all four open questions have explicit\ndispositions in the summary's Unknowns table (two resolved by investigations, two\nconverted to health warnings with measurement ACs, the rest to epic ACs); all four\nstrategy risks surface in epic scope or health warnings (plugin coupling → E001,\npath-resolution complexity → E003 scope, split-topology misconfiguration → E005's\nvalidation AC, per-request latency → E003 measurement AC + health warning). The SME\noverrides (no new flag, split deployment not config toggle, SSAR de-specified) are\nlogged and visibly honored in the epic scopes. Integration testing from the effort\nestimate is distributed as end-to-end ACs on E003 and E005 covering multi-tenant,\nbackwards-compatibility, single-tenant, fail-closed, and split-routing scenarios.\n\nConditional branches cover both bounded outcomes of E004: outcome A is delivered\nentirely within E005's routing scope (no branch epic, correctly), outcome B by\nBRANCH-B-E006 with `gate_failure_impact.action=remove`. Docs-authoring: the strategy's\nPrerequisites list `Documentation Support: _Pending human review_`, not `Yes`, so no\ndocs-authoring epic is required — the summary's Health Warning 6 documents this\nreasoning, including why no design-doc epic was generated.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":51105,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-918861ee198b169f","agent_id":"a259ae75130f24d6c","session_id":"52e2d593-99fe-4673-8bf8-9749f85fbc69","uuid":"3bd338d4-de29-4f3e-a5b7-b552565c74c4","timestamp":"2026-10-23T16:20:02.003Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
