{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8dff755cfb3b9ef","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n9\t  description: 'E005 is clearly oversized: the strategy''s own effort estimate sizes\n10\t    the operator split-deployment topology (dedicated Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute,\n11\t    artifact traffic routing) at 2-3 sprints and E005''s scope matches that work exactly\n12\t    — a multiple-sprint epic that cannot be completed in a single sprint. Split it,\n13\t    e.g. (a) dedicated artifacts-only instance deployment (Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute\n14\t    templates, workspace flag propagation, CR validation, backwards compat) and (b)\n15\t    artifact-traffic routing configuration from the main tracking deployment. Only\n16\t    (b) depends on the E004 routing-design outcome, so the split also stops E004 from\n17\t    gating the deployment-template work that does not need the routing answer.'\n18\t- severity: minor\n19\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n20\t  description: BRANCH-B-E006 scope item 'Ensure the operator's rendering of the main\n21\t    tracking deployment can set this configuration' overlaps E005's main-tracking-deployment\n22\t    routing scope under outcome B; clarify which epic delivers the operator-side wiring\n23\t    so both epics do not claim it.\n24\terror: null\n25\t---\n26\t# Decomposition Review: RHAISTRAT-8009\n27\t\n28\t## Review Summary\n29\t\n30\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n31\tRecommendation: revise\n32\t\n33\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py` exited 0 — body sections, canonical\n34\tcomponent names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, and Rules 7/8/10\n35\tparallelism all pass). Triage consistency: `triage: null` (full decomposition), so the\n36\t6-epic multi-chain output is expected and not penalized.\n37\t\n38\tThe decomposition is strong on six of seven criteria: complete HLR traceability with\n39\tcorrect priority inheritance, a coherent DAG whose frontmatter matches the summary\n40\tdiagram edge-for-edge, correct Investigation gating metadata, well-calibrated AI\n41\tsignals whose citations verify against `.context`, testable ACs, and full scope\n42\tcoverage with every risk/assumption/open question dispositioned. It fails on one\n43\tdimension: E005 bundles the entire operator split-server topology into a single epic\n44\tthat the strategy's own effort estimate sizes at 2-3 sprints — a clearly oversized,\n45\tmultiple-sprint epic that should be split.\n46\t\n47\t## Criterion Details\n48\t\n49\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n50\t\n51\tAll eight HLRs map to epics in the traceability matrix, and each epic's HLR\n52\tTraceability section confirms the mapping: the four P0 HLRs (flag composition, 403\n53\tworkspace validation, fail-closed, single-tenant/no-flags preservation) all land in\n54\tE003; the P1 operator HLRs in E005 (+ BRANCH-B-E006 for the server-side routing\n55\tportion); the P1 plugin HLR in E002 (gated by E001); the P2 audit-logging HLR in E003.\n56\t\n57\tPriority inheritance is correct in both directions. E001 and E002 are P0 because they\n58\ttransitively gate all P0 implementation work (E003) — the summary's Health Warning 1\n59\tdocuments this inversion of the strategy's P1 label on the plugin HLR explicitly. E004\n60\tis P1 and gates only P1 work (E005, BRANCH-B-E006), so no lower-priority epic blocks\n61\tP0 work. No docs-authoring epics exist, so the docs exemption is not in play.\n62\t\n63\tPriority collapse check: the P2 audit-logging HLR bundled into E003 is justified — it\n64\tis logged in the same request-handling code path that enforces the 403 denials (same\n65\tsurface, same component, same team), which is incidental polish on the P0 work, not a\n66\tdeferrable distinct feature. The summary argues exactly this. E002's P1 HLR at P0\n67\tpriority is inheritance, not collapse.\n68\t\n69\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n70\t\n71\tNo circular dependencies; no invalid edges. The mermaid diagram and epic frontmatter\n72\t`dependencies` match edge-for-edge in both directions (E001→E002, E001→E003, E002→E003,\n73\tE004→E005, E004→B006, E003→B006), with arrows drawn dependency→dependent per\n74\tconvention. Every Investigation dependency (E002/E003 on E001; E005/B006 on E004)\n75\tcarries `gated_by` pointing at an epic in that epic's direct `dependencies` list.\n76\t\n77\tBranch handling is correct: BRANCH-B-E006 sets both `branch: B` (matching the\n78\tfilename) and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E004`; no main-plan epic lists the branch epic\n79\tin its dependencies; the branch is attached as a plan document per the summary.\n80\t\n81\tThe E002→E003 edge (plugin → server) is justified: the server's artifact-request\n82\tvalidation builds against the plugin's resolution surface. The absent E003→E005 edge\n83\tis the rule-compliant shape, not a missing data dependency: the operator consumes the\n84\tworkspace-aware server as a deployed image configured with a known flag set — a\n85\tconfiguration reference (Rule 13) correctly expressed as ACs on E005 (the\n86\tcontainer-command flag-set AC and the end-to-end AC) rather than a DAG edge, keeping\n87\tthe two chains in parallel across different repos. Critical path 3 (E001→E002→E003)\n88\tis reasonable for a two-parallel-chain M-L strategy; effort is spread in width, not\n89\tdepth.\n90\t\n91\tNote (no score reduction; root cause counted under Criterion 3): the E004→E005 gate\n92\tis broader than the dependency it justifies — only E005's routing-configuration scope\n93\tdepends on the routing-design outcome, while the dedicated-instance Deployment and\n94\tService templates do not. This is a consequence of the E005 boundary problem below,\n95\tnot an independent edge defect.\n96\t\n97\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (0/2)\n98\t\n99\tComponent/team mapping is correct: `MLflow` is the only canonical component name in\n100\t`.context/rhai-components.txt` covering this stack (no `MLflow Operator` or\n101\t`mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` entries), and all epics consistently use `component:\n102\tMLflow` / `team: MLflow Team` with the mapping documented in the summary. No\n103\tcross-team bundling. No scope duplication between siblings: E002 (plugin) and E003\n104\t(server) are different codebases and surfaces; E005 (operator-side routing) and\n105\tBRANCH-B-E006 (server-side delegation config) are different surfaces of the routing\n106\toutcome. No work-product type mixing (the \"document limitations\" item in E003 is\n107\tincidental to the code change, not a content-authoring deliverable).\n108\t\n109\tHowever, **E005 is clearly oversized**. The strategy's own effort estimate sizes the\n110\toperator split-deployment topology — \"dedicated Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute,\n111\tartifact traffic routing\" — at **2-3 sprints**, and E005's scope matches that work\n112\texactly (new Helm templates for three resource types, main-deployment routing\n113\tupdates, workspace flag propagation, conditional CRD-discovery wiring, CR validation,\n114\tbackwards compatibility, and an end-to-end multi-tenant AC through the deployed split\n115\ttopology). A 2-3 sprint epic is multiple sprints of work; it cannot be completed in a\n116\tsingle sprint, so it cannot even score at the \"slightly oversized\" level. This is a\n117\tmajor issue and forces this criterion to 0.\n118\t\n119\tThe split axis is natural and the decomposition itself hints at it: only the\n120\tartifact-traffic-routing portion of E005 depends on the E004 routing-design outcome,\n121\tso E005 should be split into (a) dedicated artifacts-only instance deployment\n122\t(Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute templates, workspace flag propagation, CR validation,\n123\tbackwards compat) and (b) artifact-traffic routing configuration from the main\n124\ttracking deployment. The split also removes the over-broad E004 gate: the depl